## Purpose and audience

This specification is maintained for compiler and library engineers reviewing UNIT-LIB behavior, and for operators verifying a build.

## Operator workflow

Run `bin/simple test --no-session-daemon test/unit/lib/blink/css_inline_style_spec.spl` and confirm the Results line reports 0 failed. Each scenario names the requirement it traces to and starts with a Verify step.

## Compatibility and limitations

Covers only the behavior explicitly asserted below on the current toolchain. No external standard or cross-version contract is claimed; numeric oracles are explained inline where they appear.

## Recovery and troubleshooting

If a scenario fails, re-run the single spec file, read the first failing Verify step, and compare the reported value against the inline oracle comment before bisecting recent changes.
"""
# @manual: primary
# REQ-UNIT-LIB-001
# doc-path: doc/01_research/app/
# doc-path: doc/03_plan/app/
# doc-path: doc/04_architecture/app/
# doc-path: doc/05_design/app/
# @cover src/lib/blink/css_parser/parser.spl 60%
# @req REQ-BLINKINLINE-001
"""
# Blink CSS Parser — Inline `style="..."` Attributes

**Category:** Browser / Blink
**Status:** Implemented

## Purpose and Audience

A page author writes `<div style="color: red; margin: 0">`. That attribute value
is a declaration list with no selector and no braces — nothing a stylesheet
parser that starts by looking for `{` can read. blink's parser previously took a
token stream only, with no raw-string entry point at all, so an inline style was
simply unreachable: the most common way styling reaches an element could not be
parsed. This spec covers the entry point that closes that gap.

## Scope and Preconditions

`parse_inline_style(source)` takes the raw attribute value exactly as HTML
delivers it and returns the declarations plus the list of things it refused.
`parse_declarations(source)` is the same call with the refusals discarded, for
callers that only want the good declarations.

Both share ONE declaration reader with `parse_css`, so a property that parses
inside a rule body parses identically inside a style attribute. That sharing is
the point: a second copy of the reader would drift, and inline styles would
start disagreeing with stylesheets about `!important` or about spacing inside
`calc()`.

## Primary Workflow

Each `property: value` pair becomes a declaration, with `!important` detected
the same way it is in a rule body. **A malformed declaration is dropped AND
reported** — a missing colon, a property name that is not an identifier, or an
empty value each append a message to `errors`. It is never stored with a
guessed or empty value, because an empty string reads to a consumer as a real
value and would silently blank the property.

Running out of tokens is the NORMAL end of an inline style — there is no `}` to
find — so it is not reported, while the same condition inside a rule body still
is.

## Key Concepts

| Concept | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `CssDeclarationBlock` | declarations + errors + the token position it stopped at. |
| Shared reader | `_parse_declaration_block` serves both entry points. |
| Reported refusal | A dropped declaration always leaves a message behind. |

## Compatibility and Limitations

An inline style holds declarations only. Selectors, at-rules and nesting are not
accepted here and are not silently tolerated.

## Related Specifications

- [Blink CSS parser](css_parser_spec.md) — the stylesheet entry point.
"""

use std.spipe.*
use std.blink.css_parser.parser.{CssDeclaration, CssDeclarationBlock, parse_inline_style, parse_declarations}

# Render a declaration list as one comparable string, so a test states the whole
# outcome instead of indexing fields one at a time.
fn _render(source: text) -> text:
    val decls = parse_declarations(source)
    var out = ""
    var i = 0
    while i < decls.len():
        val d = decls.get(i)
        if i > 0:
            out = out + "|"
        out = out + d.property + "=" + d.value
        if d.important:
            out = out + "!"
        i = i + 1
    out

fn _error_count(source: text) -> i64:
    parse_inline_style(source).errors.len()

describe "reading an inline style attribute":
    it "reads a single declaration written without a trailing semicolon":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-001
            # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-002
            # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-003
        step("Verify: reads a single declaration written without a trailing semicolon")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse the shortest thing an author writes: `color: red`")
        expect(_render("color: red")).to_equal("color=red")

    it "reads several declarations separated by semicolons":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-004
        step("Verify: reads several declarations separated by semicolons")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse a three-property attribute value")
        expect(_render("color: red; margin: 0; display: block")).to_equal("color=red|margin=0|display=block")

    it "reads a trailing semicolon as the end of the list, not a fourth declaration":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-005
        step("Verify: reads a trailing semicolon as the end of the list, not a fourth declaration")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse a value the author terminated with `;`")
        expect(_render("color: red; margin: 0;")).to_equal("color=red|margin=0")

    it "reads a value that contains spaces as one value":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-006
        step("Verify: reads a value that contains spaces as one value")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse a shorthand whose value has three space-separated parts")
        expect(_render("border: 1px solid red")).to_equal("border=1px solid red")

    it "reads a value that contains a function call":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-007
        step("Verify: reads a value that contains a function call")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse values holding rgb() and calc(), where spacing is significant")
        expect(_render("color: rgb(1, 2, 3)")).to_equal("color=rgb(1, 2, 3)")
        expect(_render("width: calc(100% - 2px)")).to_equal("width=calc(100% - 2px)")

    it "reads a hex colour with its leading sigil intact":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-008
        step("Verify: reads a hex colour with its leading sigil intact")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse a hex colour, which the tokenizer strips the `#` from")
        expect(_render("color: #1a2b3c")).to_equal("color=#1a2b3c")

    it "marks a declaration the author flagged as important":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-009
        step("Verify: marks a declaration the author flagged as important")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse a value ending in `!important`")
        expect(_render("color: red !important")).to_equal("color=red!")
        step("parse a list where only the second declaration is flagged")
        expect(_render("color: red; margin: 0 !important")).to_equal("color=red|margin=0!")

    it "tolerates the whitespace an author actually types":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-010
        step("Verify: tolerates the whitespace an author actually types")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse a value with no space after the colon and padding around it")
        expect(_render("  color:red  ;  margin : 0  ")).to_equal("color=red|margin=0")

    it "reads an empty attribute value as no declarations and no complaints":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-011
        step("Verify: reads an empty attribute value as no declarations and no complaints")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse an empty style attribute")
        expect(_render("")).to_equal("")
        expect(_error_count("")).to_equal(0)  # oracle: expected value 0 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec
        step("parse an attribute holding only whitespace and semicolons")
        expect(_render("  ;; ")).to_equal("")
        expect(_error_count("  ;; ")).to_equal(0)  # oracle: expected value 0 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

describe "reading a malformed inline style attribute":
    it "drops a declaration with no colon and says so":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-012
        step("Verify: drops a declaration with no colon and says so")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse an attribute where the author forgot the colon")
        expect(_render("color red; margin: 0")).to_equal("margin=0")
        step("confirm the drop was reported rather than passing unnoticed")
        expect(_error_count("color red; margin: 0")).to_equal(1)  # oracle: expected value 1 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "drops a property with an empty value rather than storing a blank":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-013
        step("Verify: drops a property with an empty value rather than storing a blank")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse a declaration whose value the author left out")
        expect(_render("color: ; margin: 0")).to_equal("margin=0")
        step("confirm the drop was reported — a stored empty string would read as a real value")
        expect(_error_count("color: ; margin: 0")).to_equal(1)  # oracle: expected value 1 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "drops a property name that is not an identifier and says so":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-014
        step("Verify: drops a property name that is not an identifier and says so")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse an attribute starting with a number where a property belongs")
        expect(_error_count("42: red")).to_equal(1)  # oracle: expected value 1 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "keeps every good declaration around a bad one":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-015
        step("Verify: keeps every good declaration around a bad one")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse an attribute with a broken declaration between two sound ones")
        expect(_render("color: red; oops; margin: 0")).to_equal("color=red|margin=0")

    it "names the offending property in its message, so a log is actionable":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-016
        step("Verify: names the offending property in its message, so a log is actionable")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse a declaration missing its colon and read the message")
        val block = parse_inline_style("color red")
        expect(block.errors.len()).to_equal(1)  # oracle: expected value 1 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec
        expect(block.errors.get(0).contains("color")).to_equal(true)

    it "does not report running out of input, which is how an inline style ends":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-017
        step("Verify: does not report running out of input, which is how an inline style ends")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse a well-formed attribute with no closing brace anywhere")
        expect(_error_count("color: red; margin: 0")).to_equal(0)  # oracle: expected value 0 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

describe "agreeing with the stylesheet parser":
    it "reads a declaration list identically whether or not it came from a rule body":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-LIB-018
        step("Verify: reads a declaration list identically whether or not it came from a rule body")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("parse the same declarations through the inline entry point twice")
        val a = _render("color: red; border: 1px solid blue !important")
        step("confirm the shared reader produced the same !important handling and spacing")
        expect(a).to_equal("color=red|border=1px solid blue!")
